  "query_preview": "..\nThis evening and tonight: Showers and thunderstorms impacting \nnortheastern Washington and North Idaho will track to the east \nand come to an end before 6PM this evening. Gusty winds across \nthe region will also subside with quiet weather conditions \nreturning to the Inland NW overnight. \n\nMonday-Tuesday: The next low pressure settles into the Eastern \nPacific allowing downstream heights to amplify over the Inland \nNW. This will promote a warming and drying trend with \ntemperatures rebounding into the 80s to lower 90s Monday then \n90s Tuesday. Winds will be light with diurnal, terrain \ninfluence. The greatest impacts to ongoing fires will be the \nwarm and dry conditions along with increasing instability \nTuesday afternoon as mixing heights increase near 10K ft AGL or \nhigher. \n\nWednesday-Thursday: The low pressure system off the coast will \nbegin its slow journey inland. The latest consensus amongst the\nforecast models are for weaker impulses slinging out ahead of \nthe main low to come through Tuesday night and again Wednesday. \nThese impulses will help draw midlevel moisture northward \ntoward the region though the moistening will be subtle and \nforcing weak leading to low confidence for high based showers \nand thunderstorms. The first wave arrives Tuesday with a \nglancing blow to far southeastern WA and the lower Idaho \nPanhandle. The second, slightly stronger impulse comes through \nWednesday with a 5-20% chance for thunderstorms across the Idaho\nPanhandle and mountains along the International Border. \n\nThe upper-low will eventually swing inland across southwestern \nBC/NW MT Wednesday night into Thursday. Timing will be key to \nhow the winds play out Wednesday afternoon. At this time, timing\nsuggest the low will close enough by late afternoon and early \nevening for winds to increase slightly during peak heating then \ncontinue to ramp up through the evening and overnight hours as \ncooler air floods inland. This would suggest a short period of \nbreezy conditions overlapping with the hot/dry conditions. \nHowever, as previously mentioned, if timing is sooner, we could \nbe looking at another critical fire weather day. Thursday does \nlook breezy to windy across the region similar to what is \noccurring today (Sunday) as temperatures cool back from the 90s \nWednesday to 80s Thursday. Would not rule out additional showers\nand storms along the Canadian border Wednesday night into \nThursday with coverage largely isolated.\n\nFriday-Sunday: We see a lot more spread in the model guidance \nfor late week into the weekend with the evolution of the next \ntrough of low pressure. The general message is similar across \nthe board that the Pac NW will continue to be influenced by \nanother trough but how this comes through comes with many \ndifferent flavors. Some are windy while others not so much. Same\nmessage for thunderstorm threats. The greatest confidence comes\nwith temperatures being at or below normal for this time-frame \nand little to no signals widepsread precipitaiton events. /sb",
